WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.880
A rod cell in your retina can register a single photon.

2
00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.160
It reports that photon by turning a current down.

3
00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:15.540
In the dark, the rod runs a steady inward current and releases transmitter without

4
00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.780
pause. Light reduces both.

5
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.200
How can a fall carry a signal?

6
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:26.153
In the dark, the rod contains a messenger called C -G -M -P.

7
00:00:26.593 --> 00:00:31.053
It binds directly to cation channels in the membrane, with no kindness in between,

8
00:00:31.393 --> 00:00:32.633
and holds some of them open.

9
00:00:33.613 --> 00:00:37.633
Sodium and calcium flow in through those channels, and that inflow is the dark

10
00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:42.333
current. Because of it, the rod sits partly depolarized and releases glutamate

11
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:46.933
continuously. The rod must maintain this baseline all through the dark.

12
00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:53.157
Rhodopsin, the rod's receptor, holds a bent molecule called retinal.

13
00:00:53.837 --> 00:00:56.677
While retinal is bent, the receptor stays off.

14
00:00:57.177 --> 00:01:00.997
A photon straightens the retinal, and rhodopsin becomes active.

15
00:01:01.637 --> 00:01:07.057
Active rhodopsin switches on transducin, the rod's G protein, and transducin

16
00:01:07.057 --> 00:01:10.257
switches on a phosphodiesterase, PDE6.

17
00:01:10.257 --> 00:01:15.477
This enzyme destroys C -G -M -P, a messenger that was already there.

18
00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:19.730
So C -G -M -P falls, and the channels close.

19
00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.790
The inward current shrinks, and the membrane hyperpolarizes.

20
00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.350
Glutamate release drops, and the next cell reads that drop.

21
00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.310
In a toad rod, the dark current is roughly 20 picoamps.

22
00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:36.610
Each rhodopsin that a photon turns dips that current by about 1 picoamp.

23
00:01:37.383 --> 00:01:39.023
The nose works the other way round.

24
00:01:39.543 --> 00:01:44.223
Each sensory neuron in the nose has just one receptor, chosen from a very large

25
00:01:44.223 --> 00:01:50.143
family. An odorant binds its receptor, and a G protein then switches on an enzyme,

26
00:01:50.623 --> 00:01:56.723
adenylyl cyclase. The messenger cAMP rises, and it directly opens a cation channel

27
00:01:56.723 --> 00:01:57.723
related to the rod's.

28
00:01:58.343 --> 00:02:00.183
The neuron depolarizes and fires.

29
00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:04.587
In the rod, light makes C -G -M -P fall.

30
00:02:05.327 --> 00:02:08.407
In the nose, a smell makes C -A -M -P rise.

31
00:02:09.007 --> 00:02:14.947
In an artery wall, nitric oxide makes that same C -G -M -P rise, and the muscle

32
00:02:14.947 --> 00:02:17.047
relaxes. Keep this rule.

33
00:02:17.547 --> 00:02:21.767
A signal is a change from a baseline, and a cell can read a change in either

34
00:02:21.767 --> 00:02:22.307
direction.
